\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The two model shapes compared. A chat model maps prompt to answer in a single visible pass, so its mistakes tend to be surface errors an inspector can see. A reasoning model inserts a hidden thinking phase (draft, self-check, revise, converge) whose tokens are not shown to the user; the answer that emerges is more capable but the working that produced it is not available for per-step inspection.}}{83}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig-reasoning-flow}{{3.1}{83}{The two model shapes compared. A chat model maps prompt to answer in a single visible pass, so its mistakes tend to be surface errors an inspector can see. A reasoning model inserts a hidden thinking phase (draft, self-check, revise, converge) whose tokens are not shown to the user; the answer that emerges is more capable but the working that produced it is not available for per-step inspection}{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Illustrative test-time-compute scaling. Accuracy rises with the number of thinking tokens the model is allowed to spend, but with diminishing returns, and the return depends on task difficulty: the easy task plateaus early (extra thinking is wasted cost), while the hard task keeps improving over a wider range. Values are illustrative, not measured; the qualitative shape follows the published test-time-compute pattern.}}{84}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig-ttc-scaling}{{3.2}{84}{Illustrative test-time-compute scaling. Accuracy rises with the number of thinking tokens the model is allowed to spend, but with diminishing returns, and the return depends on task difficulty: the easy task plateaus early (extra thinking is wasted cost), while the hard task keeps improving over a wider range. Values are illustrative, not measured; the qualitative shape follows the published test-time-compute pattern}{figure.caption.14}{}}
