\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.1}{\ignorespaces How training-data bias propagates to a population-scale harm. Structural inequities are recorded in the data as ground-truth labels; a model learns them faithfully; deployment at scale turns the learned pattern into routine resource allocation; the under-served group is under-prioritised; and the resulting under-treatment is recorded as lower need, closing a feedback loop that reinforces the original bias.}}{240}{figure.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig-bias-propagation}{{11.1}{240}{How training-data bias propagates to a population-scale harm. Structural inequities are recorded in the data as ground-truth labels; a model learns them faithfully; deployment at scale turns the learned pattern into routine resource allocation; the under-served group is under-prioritised; and the resulting under-treatment is recorded as lower need, closing a feedback loop that reinforces the original bias}{figure.caption.31}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.2}{\ignorespaces Illustrative reconstruction of the equal-score-unequal-need pattern reported by Obermeyer et al.~(2019). At every percentile of the algorithm's risk score, Black patients carry more active chronic conditions than White patients assigned the same score; the model, trained to predict cost rather than illness, treats the two groups as equally sick. The dashed line marks the auto-referral threshold, above which patients are enrolled in a high-risk care-management program. Values are illustrative but reproduce the direction and rough magnitude of the published finding.}}{242}{figure.caption.32}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig-obermeyer-need}{{11.2}{242}{Illustrative reconstruction of the equal-score-unequal-need pattern reported by Obermeyer et al.~(2019). At every percentile of the algorithm's risk score, Black patients carry more active chronic conditions than White patients assigned the same score; the model, trained to predict cost rather than illness, treats the two groups as equally sick. The dashed line marks the auto-referral threshold, above which patients are enrolled in a high-risk care-management program. Values are illustrative but reproduce the direction and rough magnitude of the published finding}{figure.caption.32}{}}
